{"builder":"Goal: make one pull request that extends packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js to accept several files and stdin, meeting every acceptance line of https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104.\n\nContext: the CLI is packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js. Today it reads only process.argv[2]. It prints `${file}:${line}: ${error}` for each problem returned by lint(text) from packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js, with newlines in the error replaced by spaces. It exits 0 when clean, 1 when a row is bad, and 2 for no argument or an unreadable file. Tests live in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js (they spawn the bin with spawnSync) and packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js. The README is packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. CI is .github/workflows/test.yml, which runs `npm ci` then `npm test` on Node 22. Use zero dependencies, plain ES modules and node:test.\n\nSteps:\n1. Do not modify packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js in any way. lint(text) must stay byte-for-byte unchanged.\n2. Rewrite packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js:\n   - Take every argument in process.argv.slice(2), in order.\n   - With no arguments, write a usage line to stderr (e.g. `usage: jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`; use `-` for stdin) and exit 2.\n   - If `-` appears more than once, write a usage error to stderr and exit 2. Do this before checking any file.\n   - For each argument in order: if it is `-`, read stdin (readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) and use `-` as the reported name. Otherwise read the file. If a file can't be read, write `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` to stderr, remember the failure, and carry on with the remaining files. Never exit early. For every bad row, print `<file>:<line>: <error>` to stdout, exactly as the single-file output did before (the same newline-to-space replacement, and the name exactly as given).\n   - Exit code: 2 if any file could not be read. Otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad. Otherwise 0.\n   - Single-file behaviour and output must be the same as before.\n3. Extend packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js with node:test tests that spawn the bin and cover:\n   - two clean files: exit 0, empty stdout and stderr.\n   - one bad file among several clean ones: exit 1, and stdout lists only the bad file's rows as `<file>:<line>: ...`. Also add a case with bad rows in two files, checking the order follows the arguments.\n   - stdin via `-`, clean (exit 0, no output) and bad (exit 1, lines start with `-:<line>: `). Use spawnSync's `input` option.\n   - a missing file among good ones: exit 2, the stderr message names the missing file, and a bad row in another file named on the same command line is still printed on stdout.\n   - two `-` arguments: exit 2, a message on stderr.\n   - no arguments: exit 2, a usage message on stderr.\n   Keep the existing tests passing.\n4. Update packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. Under Usage, document `jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`, that files are checked in order, that `-` reads stdin and is reported as `-`, and that a second `-` is a usage error. Update Exit codes to say: 0 when every file is clean; 1 when any row in any file is bad; 2 when no file is named, `-` is given twice, or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported).\n5. Run `npm test` at the repo root and make sure it passes.\n6. Open one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. Its body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Wait for the `test` workflow at the PR head and make sure it is green. If it fails, fix it and push to the same PR.","verifier":"Check out the PR head of the pull request for https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104 and run `npm ci` at the repo root. Make fixtures in a temp dir: clean1.jsonl (`{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n`), clean2.jsonl (`{\"c\":3}\\n`), bad1.jsonl (`{\"a\":1}\\n{oops}\\n`) and bad2.jsonl (`{bad}\\n{\"x\":1}\\n{\"torn\":`). BIN means packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js. Run every command at the PR head and record its exit code, stdout and stderr.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\".\nRun `node BIN clean1.jsonl bad1.jsonl clean2.jsonl bad2.jsonl`, passing the paths exactly as you will compare them. Then run `node BIN bad2.jsonl bad1.jsonl`. For comparison, run `node BIN bad1.jsonl` and `node BIN bad2.jsonl` one at a time, both at the head and at the PR's base commit (git show <base>:packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js to a temp file next to an untouched copy of src, or use a checkout of the base).\nPASS: stdout of the multi-file run is the bad1 lines (`<path>:2: <error>`) followed by the bad2 lines (`<path>:1: ...`, `<path>:3: ...`). Each line matches what the old single-file CLI printed for that file, and the order follows the arguments (the reversed run prints the bad2 lines first).\nFAIL: a file is skipped, the order is wrong, or the line format differs from the old single-file output.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\".\nRun `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{oops}\\n' | node BIN clean1.jsonl -`, `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n' | node BIN -`, and `printf '{}\\n' | node BIN - -`.\nPASS: the first prints `-:2: <error>` and exits 1. The second prints nothing and exits 0. The third exits 2 with a message on stderr.\nFAIL: stdin is not read, it is reported under a different name, or `- -` does not exit 2.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\".\nRun `node BIN clean1.jsonl clean2.jsonl`, `node BIN clean1.jsonl bad1.jsonl`, `node BIN` with no arguments, and `node BIN clean1.jsonl missing.jsonl bad1.jsonl`.\nPASS: the exit codes are 0, 1, 2 and 2. The no-argument 